WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.008 --> 00:00:08.071
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.071 --> 00:00:12.250
One thing's very clear in this data, and
that is firms appreciate two things.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:16.760
Number one, they appreciate people who
have high ability, shall we say.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.912
People who get high grades, good test
scores, other measures of ability.

00:00:20.912 --> 00:00:23.750
The other thing they appreciate is that
you've had some experience in

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.010
their industry.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.224
Either because you learned something or
the thing we're focusing on is that you've

00:00:29.224 --> 00:00:33.398
reduced the uncertainty as to whether or
not you'd be a good fit in their industry.

00:00:33.398 --> 00:00:36.742
[MUSIC]

00:00:36.742 --> 00:00:40.152
If you've never worked in the industry
that I'm hiring you to work in before,

00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:42.880
we don't know how well you're gonna fit
there.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.750
So when I got hired into academia, they
already knew I would fit that pretty

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.090
well because I'd been a grad student and
I'd been in school a long time.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.510
But they didn't know it for sure and so
over a period of time you figure out, hey,

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.710
this person can do research and can teach
and things like that.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:02.966
It's that type of thing, and the more we
see that you've had experience doing that,

00:01:02.966 --> 00:01:06.549
the better sense we have that you will fit
in our industry.

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:09.828
[MUSIC]

00:01:09.828 --> 00:01:12.060
Let's consider two applicants for a job.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.580
They're both second year MBAs applying for
full-time jobs at the same company.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.320
Holding everything else equal,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.120
if one of those candidates has worked in
that industry before.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.330
Their odds of getting that job are more
than double

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.680
that of the person who's never worked in
that industry.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.910
In fact, they're about 2.4 times as high.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.910
So just taking, you know, as a, as a
baseline.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.010
Suppose there's a company which accepts
10% of its

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.970
applicants that have never worked in the
industry before.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.258
That same company would accept, would make
offers to about

00:01:43.258 --> 00:01:47.630
24% of the people who have worked in that
industry before.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.919
So you can see that it's quite a dramatic
difference,

00:01:49.919 --> 00:01:51.872
it's more than twice as likely to get the
job.

00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.238
[MUSIC]

00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:57.800
There's a very natural rent to own, if you
will,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.990
arrangement for MBA's, which is called the
summer internship.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.290
And it's extremely useful for testing the
waters.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:10.310
So an MBA who says, I'd really like to
work in

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.590
investment banking, but they've never
really worked in banking.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.080
They can, by going to work in banking over
the summer, they solve two problems.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.780
One, they find out whether or not they
like working in banking.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.440
And the other is,

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.790
a bank might be very reluctant to hire
somebody who's never worked in investment

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.690
banking if they're gonna have to go
through a lot of effort to fire them.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.910
They fire them naturally at the end of a
summer internship.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:36.770
There's a nice, natural breaking point in
the employment relationship.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.920
Now, a lot of those people get offers to
come back, and

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.160
that's very helpful in terms of making the
market work smoothly.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.252
But if it didn't work out for some reason,
the firm isn't stuck with

00:02:47.252 --> 00:02:51.292
a person it doesn't want, which can be
very disruptive and expensive.

00:02:51.292 --> 00:02:54.611
[MUSIC]

00:02:54.611 --> 00:02:57.672
You see that same thing where certainty,
with,

00:02:57.672 --> 00:03:02.460
where knowing that the person has
experience is valuable to that employer.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.264
But it's not quite as valuable because
they're more willing to take a chance on

00:03:06.264 --> 00:03:10.650
the outsider, because at the end of the
summer they can get rid of them for free.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.790
So in that, in, in the example there, if
we have two applicants to

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.860
a similar firm for summer internships,
the, the person with

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:26.320
experience in that industry is about 1.8
times as likely to get the job offer.

00:03:27.742 --> 00:03:32.524
Whereas if it were a full time job, it's
well over two times, closer to 2.4 times.

00:03:32.524 --> 00:03:35.844
[MUSIC]

00:03:35.844 --> 00:03:40.158
We see a lot of people who get summer
internships get an offer to go back and

00:03:40.158 --> 00:03:43.510
work there, in our data it's about half.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.500
Now a lot of the people who don't get
offers to come back that's for

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.560
budgetary reasons.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.010
They go to a small company that turns out
to not have

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.850
the ability to hire the person back or
something like that.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:57.325
But a lot of it, if you look at companies
that have large numbers of summer interns,

00:03:57.325 --> 00:04:02.000
they're making offers to maybe most of
them, but far from all of them.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.220
So firms and students are able to take
advantage of what they learned

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.170
about each other over the summer to get
the better matches.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.152
The summer is very useful, both for
learning about firms,

00:04:11.152 --> 00:04:14.470
learning about industries, learning about
each other and so forth.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:18.730
We see a lot of firms make offers to the
people that work there over the summer.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:22.270
We see a lot of students go back to those
firms, but a lot choose not to.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.350
So both sides of the market are learning
something, both about

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.780
the match they have with that firm and how
that compares to their outside options.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.967
That they then can go compare that they
then can go look at over the next

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:35.195
recruiting season.

00:04:35.195 --> 00:04:38.469
[MUSIC]

00:04:38.469 --> 00:04:40.650
The time to take a chance is in the
summer.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.600
People will take a chance on you in your
summer internships much

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.710
more than they will in full time jobs.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.950
And you see more and more, at least
Stanford students,

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.930
I don't know about other business schools
as much, taking,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:56.200
trying to split their time between two
internships over the summer.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.970
And especially if you want to try
something new,

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:02.610
that's a great idea, as long as each of
those internships is long enough for

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.940
you to learn whether or not this is the
right place for you.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.890
So if, if a five week internship is just
enough time to, you know,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.610
learn where your desk is, and you don't
really get a great feel for the industry,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.780
then that's not gonna solve the problem.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.970
But if five weeks or six weeks, or however
long a split internship is,

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:24.020
is long enough to really learn about that
industry, and that business,

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.110
then splitting your summer up gives, gives
you so many more options going forward.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.669
And so much more information going
forward.

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:40.509
[MUSIC]

